## Fit a Poisson model first

Start with Poisson to establish a baseline and check for overdispersion.

::: {#efb2909c .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
poisson_model = wk.GAM(
    "count ~ s(temperature) + s(depth)",
    family=wk.Poisson(),
).fit(data)

poisson_model.summary()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}
```
GAM fit summary
============================================================
Formula:    count ~ s(temperature) + s(depth)
Family:     Poisson(link='log')
Observations: 300
Coefficients: 19

Parametric coefficients:
  Term                       Estimate    Std.Err    z value    p-value
  ------------------------ ---------- ---------- ---------- ----------
  (Intercept)                  1.5349     0.0289     53.128    < 1e-16

Approximate significance of smooth terms:
  Term                        EDF Ref.df     Chi.sq    p-value
  ------------------------ ------ ------ ---------- ----------
  s(temperature)             4.24      5    294.642    < 1e-16
  s(depth)                   1.74      2    194.880    < 1e-16

Total EDF:  6.98
Deviance:   320.5707
Null dev:   843.4578
Dev. expl:  62.0%
GCV score:  1.120093
Scale est:  1.000000
AIC:        1305.79
BIC:        1331.65
```
:::
:::


Look at the summary's residual deviance relative to the residual degrees of freedom. A ratio
well above 1 (sometimes called the dispersion ratio) signals overdispersion. When the ratio is
large, Poisson confidence intervals are too narrow and p-values are anti-conservative.

## Fit a Negative Binomial model

Refit with `wk.NegativeBinomial()`, which estimates the extra dispersion parameter automatically.

::: {#e3d6a668 .cell execution_count=4}
``` {.python .cell-code}
nb_model = wk.GAM(
    "count ~ s(temperature) + s(depth)",
    family=wk.NegativeBinomial(),
).fit(data)

nb_model.summary()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}
```
GAM fit summary
============================================================
Formula:    count ~ s(temperature) + s(depth)
Family:     NegativeBinomial(theta=329.8, link='log')
Observations: 300
Coefficients: 19

Parametric coefficients:
  Term                       Estimate    Std.Err    z value    p-value
  ------------------------ ---------- ---------- ---------- ----------
  (Intercept)                  1.5347     0.0291     52.789    < 1e-16

Approximate significance of smooth terms:
  Term                        EDF Ref.df     Chi.sq    p-value
  ------------------------ ------ ------ ---------- ----------
  s(temperature)             4.24      5    290.499    < 1e-16
  s(depth)                   1.73      2    190.879    < 1e-16

Total EDF:  6.97
Deviance:   315.5892
Null dev:   829.7223
Dev. expl:  62.0%
GCV score:  1.102575
Scale est:  1.000000
AIC:        1305.71
BIC:        1331.52
```
:::
:::


Compare the deviance explained between the two models. The Negative Binomial typically shows a
lower (more honest) deviance explained because it no longer tries to explain variance that is
simply inherent noise. The estimated dispersion parameter appears in the summary. A value near
zero indicates little overdispersion, a large value indicates substantial overdispersion.

